###

### NOTES:
### 1. Update the @baseUrl variable with your actual development server URL and port
### 2. The service URL is: {baseUrl}/AppointmentService.svc
### 3. Correlation Headers - THREE ways to provide them:
###    a) HTTP Headers: X-Correlation-Id, X-Consumer-Id, X-User-Id
###    b) Root Level Body: ConsumerId, UserId, CorrelationId (as direct properties)
###    c) Nested Header Object: Inside "header" or "headers" object in request body
### 4. Extraction Priority:
###    - HTTP Headers are checked first
###    - If not found, checks root level of request body
###    - If still not found, checks nested "header" or "headers" object
###    - CorrelationId is auto-generated if not provided anywhere
### 5. Supported Content Types:
###    - JSON: application/json (fully flexible)
###    - XML: application/xml or text/xml (must use WCF namespace and PascalCase)
### 6. XML Requirements (WCF DataContractSerializer):
###    - Must include namespace: xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/RedactionWcf.Models"
###    - Element names must match DataMember properties EXACTLY (PascalCase)
###    - Elements are serialized in ALPHABETICAL order by WCF
###    - camelCase XML will NOT work without additional configuration
### 7. Supported nested object/element names (case-insensitive for extraction):
###    JSON: "header" or "Header", "headers" or "Headers"
###    XML: <Header>, <Headers> (must be PascalCase for WCF)
### 8. Supported property/element names (case-insensitive for extraction):
###    - CorrelationId or correlationId
###    - ConsumerId or consumerId  
###    - UserId or userId
### 9. Check Visual Studio Output window (Debug) to see logged requests/responses
### 10. All requests and responses are logged with correlation tracking
### 11. Sensitive data (passwords, tokens, API keys) are automatically redacted
### 12. To run tests:
###     - Click "Send Request" above each request in Visual Studio
###     - Response will appear in a new editor window
### 13. Response will include X-Correlation-Id header for tracking

### EXAMPLES SUMMARY:
### Examples 1-3: HTTP Headers only (JSON)
### Examples 4-6: Various combinations (JSON)
### Examples 7-11: Testing error cases and features (JSON)
### Examples 12-16: Nested header object patterns (JSON)
### Examples 17-24: XML Request Examples (WCF Compatible)
###    - Example 17: Root level correlation IDs in XML
###    - Example 18: Nested <Header> element
###    - Example 19: Nested <Headers> element (Plural)
###    - Example 20: HTTP headers override XML body
###    - Example 21: Mixed levels (some nested, some root)
###    - Example 22: Auto-generated correlation ID
###    - Example 23: Complete headers with metadata
###    - Example 24: Minimal required fields only

### IMPORTANT: XML elements are shown in alphabetical order (WCF requirement)
### The logging module will extract correlation IDs regardless of element order
